--- a/resources/language/resource.language.en_gb/strings.po
+++ b/resources/language/resource.language.en_gb/strings.po
@@ -1181,7 +1181,7 @@ msgid "Off (the default): run from a USB drive, this system does not see the dis
 msgstr ""
 
 msgctxt "#32924"
-msgid "With this setting on, from the next start:[CR][CR]- This system sees the disks inside the computer and mounts them read-only: all of them when it runs from a USB drive; when it is installed on one of them, the other disks and the other partitions of its own disk. That is where Windows and your files are.[CR][CR]- Everything that runs here can read them: the media centre and every add-on, also the ones made by other people. With file sharing (Samba) turned on, they can be shared on your network too.[CR][CR]- \"Read-only\" is how the disks are mounted, not a lock. Add-ons run with full rights: one that wanted to could mount them again for writing.[CR][CR]- Below this setting you can choose disks that are kept away from the kernel, which not even a program that runs here with full rights can then reach, and partitions that are not mounted, which such a program could still read.[CR][CR]- The kernel runs without its lockdown, unless a disk is kept away. In the normal start it loads only the drivers that came with this system and keeps programs away from the memory and the hardware of the computer. In this one it does neither.[CR][CR]- It is so on every computer that this drive is started on.[CR][CR]It stays on until you turn it off here, reset this system to its defaults, or choose \"run\" once in the menu that is shown when the computer starts (on a UEFI computer that entry is \"Run\", not the one that names the internal disks). An update made with the installer for Windows turns it off as well."
+msgid "With this setting on, from the next start:[CR][CR]- This system sees the disks inside the computer and mounts them read-only: all of them when it runs from a USB drive; when it is installed on one of them, the other disks and the other partitions of its own disk. That is where Windows and your files are.[CR][CR]- Everything that runs here can read them: the media centre and every add-on, also the ones made by other people. With file sharing (Samba) turned on, they can be shared on your network too.[CR][CR]- \"Read-only\" is how the disks are mounted, not a lock. Add-ons run with full rights: one that wanted to could mount them again for writing.[CR][CR]- Below this setting you can choose disks that are kept away from the kernel, which not even a program that runs here with full rights can then reach, and partitions that are not mounted, which such a program could still read.[CR][CR]- The kernel runs without its lockdown, unless a disk is kept away, and lets every device reach all memory. In the normal start it loads only the drivers that came with this system, keeps programs away from the memory and the hardware of the computer and, where the computer can, lets each device reach only the memory it is given. In this one it does none of that.[CR][CR]- It is so on every computer that this drive is started on.[CR][CR]It stays on until you turn it off here, reset this system to its defaults, or choose \"run\" once in the menu that is shown when the computer starts (on a UEFI computer that entry is \"Run\", not the one that names the internal disks). An update made with the installer for Windows turns it off as well."
 msgstr ""
 
 msgctxt "#32925"
@@ -1569,7 +1569,7 @@ msgid "Every internal disk is shown from the next start"
 msgstr ""
 
 msgctxt "#33157"
-msgid "With disks kept away, from the next start:[CR][CR]- Before anything else runs, this system lets go of the disks that are not ticked. Kodi, its add-ons and file sharing do not see them, and neither can a program that runs here with full rights reach them.[CR][CR]- The kernel attaches no other internal disk until the next start: a disk that is plugged in while the system runs is seen from the next start.[CR][CR]- The kernel runs with its lockdown, as in the normal start: it loads only the drivers that came with this system, not the ones that add-ons bring, and keeps programs away from the memory and the hardware of the computer.[CR][CR]- A disk with no serial number, or with the same one as another disk, cannot be told apart from others, so it is kept away too, and every disk is kept away when the list cannot be read. The disk this system is on is never kept away.[CR][CR]- Where a disk that is to be kept away cannot be (the system cannot let go of it, or cannot tell which disk it starts from), it stays, and no partition of an internal disk is mounted in that start. That is said when Kodi starts.[CR][CR]- It holds against what runs in this system, until the next start. It does not hold against a program that changes this list, the boot menu or what the system runs as it starts (scripts on the partition LIBREELEC, an update in /storage), and waits for the next start.[CR][CR]- It is so on every computer that this drive is started on: their disks go with the first line of the list."
+msgid "With disks kept away, from the next start:[CR][CR]- Before anything else runs, this system lets go of the disks that are not ticked. Kodi, its add-ons and file sharing do not see them, and neither can a program that runs here with full rights reach them.[CR][CR]- The kernel attaches no other internal disk until the next start: a disk that is plugged in while the system runs is seen from the next start.[CR][CR]- The kernel runs with its lockdown, as in the normal start: it loads only the drivers that came with this system, not the ones that add-ons bring, and keeps programs away from the memory and the hardware of the computer. Unlike the normal start, it does not keep each device to the memory it is given (that can only be asked for as the computer starts), so a device whose firmware a program has replaced could still reach the memory of the kernel.[CR][CR]- A disk with no serial number, or with the same one as another disk, cannot be told apart from others, so it is kept away too, and every disk is kept away when the list cannot be read. The disk this system is on is never kept away.[CR][CR]- Where a disk that is to be kept away cannot be (the system cannot let go of it, or cannot tell which disk it starts from), it stays, and no partition of an internal disk is mounted in that start. That is said when Kodi starts.[CR][CR]- It holds against what runs in this system, until the next start. It does not hold against a program that changes this list, the boot menu or what the system runs as it starts (scripts on the partition LIBREELEC, an update in /storage), and waits for the next start.[CR][CR]- It is so on every computer that this drive is started on: their disks go with the first line of the list."
